The `deleteAgent` action function receives `allPolicies` (Onyx-connected data) as a parameter from the UI component. Action functions in `src/libs/actions/` should subscribe to Onyx data directly via `Onyx.connect` at the module level rather than receiving it as a function argument. This pattern couples the action to whoever calls it and forces callers to subscribe to data they do not need for rendering.

Subscribe to the policy collection at module level:

```ts
let allPolicies: OnyxCollection<Policy> = {};
Onyx.connect({
    key: ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY,
    waitForCollectionCallback: true,
    callback: (val) => (allPolicies = val),
});

function deleteAgent(accountID: number, agentEmail?: string) {
    // ... use module-scoped allPolicies instead of parameter
}
```

`useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY)` is subscribed here solely to pass `allPolicies` to the `deleteAgent` action function. The component never uses policy data for its own rendering or logic -- it is acting as a pure data intermediary. This also causes the component to re-render on every policy collection change, which is wasteful.

The `deleteAgent` action in `src/libs/actions/Agent.ts` should subscribe to the policy collection internally via `Onyx.connect` (or use `OnyxUtils.multiGet`) rather than receiving it as a parameter. This keeps the data dependency local to the action that needs it and avoids unnecessary re-renders in the UI component.

```ts
// In src/libs/actions/Agent.ts
let allPolicies: OnyxCollection<Policy> = {};
Onyx.connect({
    key: ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY,
    waitForCollectionCallback: true,
    callback: (val) => (allPolicies = val),
});

function deleteAgent(accountID: number, agentEmail?: string) {
    // Use module-scoped allPolicies directly
}
```

Same pattern as `SubmitPrimaryAction`: the outer `SubmitActionButton` fetches ~10 Onyx values and other hook results solely to pass them as 18 props to `SubmitActionButtonContent`. The content component previously owned its own behavior.

Keep data-fetching hooks inside the content component and only pass the minimal coordination props (`iouReportID`, animation callbacks) to the wrapper:
```tsx
function SubmitActionButton({iouReportID, ...animationProps}: SubmitActionButtonProps) {
    return (
        <ReportSubmitToPopoverAnchor reportID={iouReportID} onSubmitSuccess={animationProps.startSubmittingAnimation}>
            <SubmitActionButtonContent iouReportID={iouReportID} {...animationProps} />
        </ReportSubmitToPopoverAnchor>
    );
}
```

The refactored `SubmitPrimaryAction` acts as a pure data intermediary -- it fetches ~12 Onyx values and other hook results solely to pass them as 26 props to `SubmitPrimaryActionContent`. The parent does not use any of this data for its own rendering logic. The content component previously owned its own behavior (fetched its own data), and this refactoring moves all data fetching to the parent just to wrap with `ReportSubmitToPopoverAnchor`.

Instead, keep the data-fetching hooks inside the content component and place the `ReportSubmitToPopoverAnchor` wrapper at a higher level or pass only the minimal coordination props (`reportID`, `onSubmitSuccess`) that the anchor actually needs:
```tsx
function SubmitPrimaryAction({reportID}: SubmitPrimaryActionProps) {
    const {startSubmittingAnimation} = usePaymentAnimationsContext();
    return (
        <ReportSubmitToPopoverAnchor reportID={reportID} onSubmitSuccess={startSubmittingAnimation}>
            <SubmitPrimaryActionContent reportID={reportID} />
        </ReportSubmitToPopoverAnchor>
    );
}
```
Then let `SubmitPrimaryActionContent` call `useOnyx`, `useCurrentUserPersonalDetails`, etc. internally, as it did before.

`childReport` is fetched via `useOnyx` in `PureReportActionItem` but is only used to pass it through to `ExportIntegration` (line 884). This makes the parent a data intermediary -- it imports a subscription it does not use for its own logic. Every change to the child report will trigger a re-render of the entire `PureReportActionItem` component tree, even though the data is only needed in one conditional branch.

`ExportIntegration` already receives `action`, which contains `childReportID`. Move the `useOnyx` call into `ExportIntegration` (or into `ReportActionItemMessageWithExplain`) so the component that actually needs the data owns the subscription:

```tsx
// In ExportIntegration.tsx
function ExportIntegration({action, originalReport}: ExportIntegrationProps) {
    const [childReport] = useOnyx(`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT}${getNonEmptyStringOnyxID(action?.childReportID)}`);
    // ...
}
```

This removes the `childReport` prop from `ExportIntegrationProps` and avoids unnecessary re-renders of `PureReportActionItem`.

`CameraViewport` receives `boltIcon` as a prop from the parent, which fetches it via `useMemoizedLazyExpensifyIcons` only to pass it down. The component should call `useMemoizedLazyExpensifyIcons(['Bolt'])` internally instead, removing one prop from the interface.

```tsx
function CameraViewport({...}: CameraViewportProps) {
    const lazyIcons = useMemoizedLazyExpensifyIcons(['Bolt']);
    // Use lazyIcons.Bolt instead of boltIcon prop
}
```

`ScannerControlsBar` receives 5 icon/illustration props (`shutterIllustration`, `galleryIcon`, `receiptMultipleIcon`, `boltIcon`, `boltSlashIcon`) that are fetched by the parent via `useMemoizedLazyExpensifyIcons` and `useMemoizedLazyIllustrations` solely to pass them down. The parent does not use these icons for its own rendering. The component should call these hooks internally to load its own assets, eliminating 5 props from the interface.

```tsx
function ScannerControlsBar({...}: ScannerControlsBarProps) {
    const lazyIllustrations = useMemoizedLazyIllustrations(['Shutter']);
    const lazyIcons = useMemoizedLazyExpensifyIcons(['Bolt', 'Gallery', 'ReceiptMultiple', 'boltSlash']);
    // Use lazyIllustrations.Shutter, lazyIcons.Gallery, etc. directly
}
```

The parent passes `wasSubmittedViaHarvesting` as a prop to `ReportActionItemMessageWithExplain`, but this is information the child component can derive on its own. The child already receives the `message` prop (which is `translate('iou.automaticallySubmitted')` containing an HTML anchor tag) and already imports `CONST.REGEX_ANCHOR_WITH_TEXT` to parse it. The presence of an anchor tag in the message is sufficient to determine the rendering path.

Additionally, this code is inside an `if (wasSubmittedViaHarvesting)` block (line 1347), so the prop is always `true` when passed — making it doubly redundant.

Suggested fix: Remove the `wasSubmittedViaHarvesting` prop from this call site. Let the child component detect the anchor tag in the `message` and decide its rendering behavior internally:

```tsx
children = (
    <ReportActionItemMessageWithExplain
        message={translate('iou.automaticallySubmitted')}
        action={action}
        childReport={childReport}
        originalReport={originalReport}
    />
);
```

